<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>{{ title }}</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="../style.css">
</head>
<body>

  <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
  
  <h2>Preamble</h2>

  <p class="body">This Full Disclosure Report (FDR) documents the results obtained from a single execution of the Kubernetes 
  Autoscaling Benchmark (KASBench) conducted from {{ start_date }} to {{ end_date }}.  
  In this evaluation, the following autoscaler configurations were tested:
  </p>

  <ul>
  {% for item in autoscaler_list %}
      <li>{{ item }}</li>
  {% endfor %}
  </ul>


  <p>
  The purpose of this report is to completely document the benchmark environment, execution procedures, system
  configuration, and observed results of a full KASBench execution to enable independent verification and
  reproduction.</p>

  <p class="body">
  KASBench is a comparative benchmark intended to evaluate Kubernetes autoscaling systems under controlled workloads
  and documented conditions.  It measures autoscaler performance using five metrics  
    ({{- theta_s -}}, {{ theta_a -}}, {{ tau_s -}}, {{ tau_a -}}, and {{ bar_c -}})
  and three composite scores
    ({{- d_s -}}, {{ d_a -}}, and {{ zeta -}}). 
  These metrics and composite scores measure deviation from the ideal, and are in the range 0 (best) to 100 (worst) inclusive.  Lower
  scores represent autoscaler performance closer to the ideal.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
  <strong>Synchronous underprovisioning penalty ({{-theta_s-}})</strong> quantifies the mean deviation above a pre-determined service level objective 
  (SLO) for synchronous requests initiated by a synthetic load generator over the benchmark period.
  Results are calculated as a percentage in the range 0 to 100, inclusive, using an upper bound of {{ k_word -}}-times the SLO.
  Sub-metrics for response-time ({{-theta_rt_s-}}) and failure-rate ({{-theta_fr_s-}}) are blended to calculate {{-theta_s-}}.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
  <strong>Synchronous underprovisioned time slices ({{-tau_s-}})</strong> measures the percentage of {{ time_slice_seconds -}}-second
  time slices during which requests exceed the SLO by more than {{ tolerance_percent -}}%.  It is calculated by blending
  the sub-metrics for response-time ({{-tau_rt_s-}}) and failure-rate ({{-tau_fr_s-}}).
  </p>

  <p class="body">
  <strong>Asynchronous underprovisioning penalty ({{- theta_a -}})</strong> quantifies the how long asynchronous tasks wait 
  in queue beyond a predetermined baseline ({{- w_max }} second{{- 's' if w_max != 1 else '' -}}). Asynchronous tasks are triggered indirectly
  from synchronous requests. Results are calculated as a percentage in the range 0 to 100, inclusive, using an upper bound of 
  {{ k_word -}}-times the SLO.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
  <strong>Asynchronous underprovisioned time slices ({{-tau_a-}})</strong> measures the percentage of {{ time_slice_seconds -}}-second
  time slices during which asynchronous tasks exceed the baseline by more than {{ tolerance_percent -}}%. 
  </p>

  <p class="body">
  <strong>Blended mean resource utilization ({{- bar_c -}})</strong> is the blended percentage of available CPU and memory 
  requested by each autoscaler in excess of a baseline.  The baseline is calculated as the minimum resources that could be allocated by any
  autoscaler in the test.  
  </p>


  <p class="body">

  The metrics {{theta_s-}}, {{tau_s-}}, {{theta_a-}}, {{tau_a-}}, and {{ bar_c }} represent deviation from the ideal on a scale of 0 to 100, inclusive.

  </p>


  <p class="body">
  <strong>Derived synchronous composite score ({{- d_s -}})</strong> is the weighted Minkowski deviation (distance) of 
  {{ theta_s }} and {{ tau_s }} from zero.
  </p>
  
  <p class="body">
  <strong>Derived asynchronous composite score ({{- d_a -}})</strong> is the weighted Minkowski deviation (distance) of 
  {{ theta_a }} and {{ tau_a }} from zero.
  </p>
  </p>

  <p class="body">
  <strong>Composite score ({{- zeta -}})</strong> is the weighted Minkowski distance of {{ d_s }}, {{ d_a }}, and {{ bar_c -}}.
  </p>

  <p class="body">
  The evaluation utilizes a completely random design.  Each benchmark evaluation comprises multiple trials.  The number of 
  trials per autoscaler is indicated in the <em>Metadata</em> section of this document.  Trials are independent.  Each trial
  is conducted in an identical, individually built, one-time-use virtual private cloud in a randomly selected Amazon Web Services (AWS)
  availability zone.  Autoscalers under test are randomly assigned to a trial and AWS availability zone.  
  </p>


  <h2>Autoscalers Under Test</h2>

  This section describes the configuration of each autoscaler under test.  Autoscalers are expected to be configured optimally for the benchmark
  evaluation, including resource requests and limits, number of replicas, and autoscaler-specific configuration.

  {% for autoscaler in autoscaler_descriptions %}

  <h3>{{ autoscaler["name"] -}}</h3>

  <p class="body">
  {{ autoscaler["description"] }}
  </p>

  {% endfor %}

